"05_point_rect.mst — Point's rectangle-making protocol (DolphinDart DD9).

 `st/world/28_point.mst` says of `extent:` / `corner:` / `insetRect:` /
 `expandRect:`:

     DELIBERATELY NOT ported — they return/accept a Rectangle, Strongtalk's own
     UI-toolkit geometry class, which MACVM has no use for at all: the GUI is
     HTML/JS-rendered, not Smalltalk-Rectangle-based.

 That was the right call for MACVM. **This project reverses it**: the goal is
 Dolphin's MVP GUI, `BorderLayout` computes in Rectangles, and
 `left @ top extent: width @ height` is how every one of its five branches
 builds one. So the protocol comes back — in the compat layer rather than in
 the world, because the world's rationale still holds for the world.

 `Rectangle` itself is NOT reimplemented here: it is Dolphin's own
 `Graphics.Rectangle`, translated into `st/mvp`. This file is only the two
 constructors that live on the Point side, and they are Dolphin's own bodies.

 `Rectangle` is unbound when this file loads (st/mvp comes later in the layer
 order) and that is fine — the name is resolved when the method RUNS, the same
 way every reference to `Win32` in this layer is."
Object subclass: Point [
    "Dolphin: `^Rectangle origin: self extent: aPoint`."
    extent: aPoint [ ^Rectangle origin: self extent: aPoint ]

    "Dolphin: `^Rectangle origin: self corner: aPoint`."
    corner: aPoint [ ^Rectangle origin: self corner: aPoint ]

    "Dolphin's `Point>>rect:` answers the rectangle spanning the two points,
     normalised so origin is the top-left whichever order they arrive in."
    rect: aPoint [
        ^Rectangle
            origin: ((self x min: aPoint x) @ (self y min: aPoint y))
            corner: ((self x max: aPoint x) @ (self y max: aPoint y))
    ]
]
